// Bun Snapshot v1, https://bun.sh/docs/test/snapshots

exports[`armada --help help preserves its output 1`] = `
"armada VERSION: launch coding agents in provider-scoped sandboxes

Bare command families print help on stdout and exit 0; unknown subcommands exit 2.

Global: --profile NAME (or ARMADA_PROFILE) isolates saved login and preferences; login --url sets its server.
--profile overrides ARMADA_PROFILE. Omit both for the existing default profile.
Names: 1–64 letters/digits/underscores/hyphens, starting with a letter or digit.
ARMADA_TOKEN is not isolated by profiles: it still overrides every saved login.
Use a matching ARMADA_URL for token-authenticated scripts; never put tokens in arguments.
Example: armada --profile staging login --url https://staging.example.com --provider github

Exit codes: 0 success; 1 server/operation failure; 2 invalid usage or missing configuration; 3 authentication failure.
Scripts: check exit status before consuming stdout; failed operations may have partial output.
Common typed errors with --json use {error:{code,message}} on stderr, not stdout.
Progress/warnings can also use stderr; do not assume the entire stream is one JSON document.
See command --help for supported JSON output; keep captured values/tokens out of shared logs.

Quick start / common examples:
  armada login --email you@example.com
  armada org list
  armada org use my-team
  armada doctor
  armada host add --name my-workstation
  armada launch
  armada list
  armada status SANDBOX_ID
  armada send SANDBOX_ID "Continue the task"
  armada logs SANDBOX_ID --tail 20
  armada stop SANDBOX_ID
  eval "$(armada completion bash)"

Usage:
  armada host install --server HTTPS_ORIGIN   Install on the Docker machine; one-use token on stdin, no login
  armada host status|uninstall [--server HTTPS_ORIGIN]  Local host wrappers; no login
  armada host add [--name NAME] [--org UUID]
  armada host list [--json] [--org UUID]
  armada host show HOST [--json] [--org UUID]
  armada host limits HOST [--cpus N] [--memory SIZE] [--max-sandboxes N | --auto] [--org UUID]
  armada host retention HOST --retention 24h [--org UUID]
  armada host revoke UUID [--org UUID]
  armada launch [prompt] --provider docker [--host UUID|NAME | --golden snapshot-UUID] --agent claude|codex|opencode [--credential-id UUID] [--org NAME|UUID] [--repo owner/repo[@branch] ...] [--variable KEY:UUID:VERSION ...] [--secret KEY:UUID:VERSION ...] [--request-id UUID] [--json]
                                           Docker launch through the normal Armada API using the official Armada environment.
  armada doctor [--org UUID] [--json]      Check session, server, GitHub, hosts and model accounts
  armada completion bash|zsh|fish           Print a shell completion script
  armada --version                         Print the version
  armada --help                            Show this help (also -h or help); no login required
  armada skill install (--project <directory> | --global) --yes [--revision <commit>]
                                           Public GitHub Armada tool-knowledge skill; no login. See skill --help.
  armada snapshot create <name> (--repo <url>[@branch] ... | --config <file>) [--init <cmd> ...]
                               [--setup <local-file>] [--provider boat|modal] [--private] [--org <org>] [--idempotency-key <key>] [--timeout <seconds>]
                                           Server-orchestrated: builds, scans and saves the snapshot on the
                                           server over your own GitHub connection. No BOAT_API_KEY needed.
                                           --setup reads a local script and sends its content to run after
                                           cloning, before the scan.
  armada snapshot update <name> (--add-repo <url>[@branch] ... | --replace --repo <url>[@branch] ... | --replace --config <file>) [--init <cmd> ...]
                               [--setup <local-file>] [--provider boat|modal] [--org <org>] [--idempotency-key <key>] [--timeout <seconds>]
                                           Rebuild the existing snapshot in place. One snapshot may contain
                                           many repositories; visibility and ownership stay unchanged.
                                           Use --add-repo to append safely, preserving stored repositories and init commands.
                                           --repo/--config require --replace: they replace the ENTIRE config, even if no repos are dropped.
                                           Create/update admit a durable job, then poll for up to --timeout
                                           seconds (default 60, max 600; 0 returns after admission).
                                           Disconnecting never resubmits or cancels the job.
  armada snapshot build-cancel <job-id> [--org <org>] [--json]  Cancel your queued, never-started build
  armada snapshot build-find <name> [--provider boat|modal] [--org <org>] [--json]
                                           Find the latest visible active build without a job ID or admission key.
  armada snapshot build-status <job-id>      Read the authenticated durable build status
  armada snapshot build-status --idempotency-key <key> --org <org>
                                           Recover an uncertain admission using the saved key.
  armada snapshot capabilities [--provider boat|modal] [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Server-authoritative snapshot capacity; null means unbounded/unknown
  armada snapshot size-policy <name> [--provider boat|modal] [--org <org>] [--file <JSON|->] [--json]
                                           Inspect defaults, or replace the entire policy from a file/stdin.
                                           Version 1: {"version":1,"golden":null,"repositories":{}}
                                           Repository keys are configured directories. A null default and an empty map
                                           clear defaults. Scoped to this provider snapshot, future launches only.
  armada snapshot create <name> --provider docker --host UUID|NAME --org UUID
                               (--config <file> | --repo <url>[@branch] ... [--init <cmd> ...]) [--private] [--request-id UUID] [--json]
                                           Build a host-local snapshot from the official Armada image and your repos/setup.
                                           No image input. Reuse --request-id with identical input after an unknown outcome.
  armada snapshot list [--provider boat|modal|docker] [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Snapshots you can see; Docker requires --org.
  armada snapshot get UUID --provider docker --org UUID [--json]
                                           Read snapshot build state; launch ready snapshots with launch --provider docker --agent codex --golden UUID.
  armada snapshot rebuild UUID --provider docker --org UUID [--request-id UUID] [--json]
                                           Build a new snapshot from saved repos/setup on its original host; keep the old one.
  armada launch <snapshot> --agent claude|codex|opencode ["prompt"] [--model <id>] [--reasoning-effort <level>]
                                           [--armada-access [--credential-id <id>] [--max-active-children <1..100>]]
                [--provider boat|modal] [--repo <configured-dir|owner/repo>] [--ttl 4h] [--json]
                [--skill org/slug@version|slug@version ...] [--connections <id,...> | --no-connections] [--org <org>]
                Omit connections to request server defaults; --no-connections opts out.
                                           Fork a snapshot into a sandbox. With a prompt the agent starts at once;
                                           without one the sandbox waits, ready, for the first prompt. The model
                                           and effort apply to every later turn too. --credential-id UUID selects a model account;
                                           otherwise use your personal or organization default.
                                           --repo selects the native agent's initial working directory, including when idle.
                                           Defaults:
                                           claude claude-opus-5-5 medium, codex gpt-6.2-sol medium, opencode armada/deepseek-flash default
  armada list [--status STATUS] [--agent|--harness claude|codex|opencode] [--golden UUID] [--owner UUID|me]
              [--page N] [--limit 1..200] [--org UUID] [--json] [--page-info]
                                           Default page size 25; --json keeps {sandboxes:[...]}; --page-info adds exact totals
  armada mcp sandbox-connections <sandbox-uuid> [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Read actual bound connection metadata, never mutate bindings
  armada advice <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Last-10 comparable-session resource evidence; collects one bounded sample.
                                           Missing/partial evidence abstains. Never resizes or changes defaults.
                                           Human launch guidance uses stderr only; --json launch never requests advice.
  armada bind-credential <sandbox-id> --credential-id UUID [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Owner-only legacy Modal binding recovery; stop safely first; no restart
  armada rm <sandbox-id> --yes [--org UUID] [--json]  Permanently delete a stopped sandbox and its recovery archives
  armada extend <sandbox-id> --ttl <duration> [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           TTL is measured from now, not added to the old deadline; must extend it.
  armada status <sandbox-id> [--live] [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Recorded state by default (no provider probe); --live reads provider/prompt details
  armada detail|recovery|stop|interrupt <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
  armada wait <sandbox-id> [--timeout <seconds>] [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Timeout is 0–600 seconds, default 60.
  armada report <sandbox-id> ready|blocked --prompt-id ID [--reason TEXT] [--pr-number N] [--pr-url URL] [--head-sha SHA]
  armada send|steer|exec <sandbox-id> <text-or-command> [--model ID] [--reasoning-effort LEVEL]
                                           send accepts --request-id UUID to reuse an uncertain runtime instruction.
                                           Full-account follow-up sends can queue FIFO; queued is not completed.
  armada api ROUTE [JSON|-]
  armada snapshot retirement dry-run|reconcile <snapshot-id> [--limit 1-50] [--org UUID]
  armada snapshot remove <name|snapshot-id> [--provider boat|modal] [--org UUID] [--json]
                                           Names must match one visible snapshot; use provider/org or snapshot list --json to disambiguate.
                                           Remove from launch selection; retain children and provider artifacts
  armada snapshot visibility <snapshot-id> --visibility private|org [--org UUID]
  armada mcp catalog|rename ID NAME|default ID on|off [--org UUID]
  armada mcp add NAME --service notion|linear [--scope personal|org] [--org UUID]
  armada mcp add NAME URL [--scope personal|org] [--org UUID]
                                           Public/OAuth URL shortcut; auto discovery, no secret argv
  armada mcp list|add|update|connect|status|reconnect|enable|disable|revoke|delete [id] [--org UUID] [--file JSON]
  armada logs <sandbox-id> [--tail [n]] [--all] [--follow] [--json]
                                           Read agent events; --all drains available pages, --follow waits for new pages until settled
  armada login [--url <url>] [--provider github|google | --email <address>]
                                           Discovers enabled methods; prompts for email when available.
                                           --email: open the emailed link on this machine, or type its code (use one, not both)
  armada logout
  armada token create [--org <name>] [--label <label>]
  armada token list
  armada token revoke <id>
  armada org create <name>                 Create an org; you are its first admin
  armada org list
  armada org setup [--org <name>]          Show organization provider setup status
  armada org connect boat|modal [--org <name>] [--file <path>]
                                           Connect an org provider using hidden input, stdin or a file
  armada org use <name|UUID|none>           Persist selection for this Armada server
  armada org current                        Show selected organization
  armada account status|logout              Account identity / revoke current login session
  armada github status|connect|reconnect|disconnect|install|manage [installation-id]
  armada github refresh <sandbox>
                                           Browser authorization waits for real completion; --no-browser prints URL
  armada org invitations create <email> [--role admin|member] [--org <name|UUID>] [--json]
  armada org invitations list [--org <name|UUID>] [--json]
  armada org invitations resend|revoke <id> [--org <name|UUID>] [--json]
                                           Admin invitation management; --org overrides org use selection.
                                           Email sent means provider accepted, not confirmed inbox delivery.
  armada org invitations accept [--file <path>] [--json]
                                           Token from stdin/file only; log in personally with the matching
                                           verified email first. Opening a link never joins an organization.
  armada org members list|add|remove|role  [--org <name>] add <email> [--role admin|member]
                                           Direct membership administration; add requires an existing account,
                                           grants membership immediately and does not send an invitation.
  armada creds login codex --name <name> [--org <name>]
                                           Native local device login; up to 16 minutes after the device code.
                                           Keep this terminal open; Ctrl-C/shell exit cancels.
  armada creds import claude|codex --name <name> [--file <path>] [--org <name>]
                                           Import a locally obtained native token/auth.json; never pass secrets as arguments
  armada creds limits <account-id> [--model <model-id>] --max-agents N | --unlimited [--org <name>]
                                           Set the maximum simultaneous running agents for a model account
  armada creds presets [--json]             List the same model connection providers as the dashboard
  armada creds connect <preset> --name NAME [--file PATH] [--org NAME]
                                           Provider key from hidden input, stdin/file; custom providers take --models and --base-url
                                           Azure models are resource deployment names; official Azure addresses are tested
                                           with a tiny billable request per deployment. A failed check saves nothing.
  armada creds set <kind> [--org <name>] [--file <path>]
                                           Store an encrypted credential; use hidden terminal input, stdin or a file
                                           Model accounts: --max-agents N or --unlimited (Morph defaults to 2; others unlimited)
                                           Model accounts: --name NAME creates; --id UUID --expected-version N replaces
                                           Codex: --base-url URL --models deployment,model [--api-version VERSION]
                                           Key from hidden input, stdin/file; Azure requires actual deployment names, not model catalog IDs
                                           Codex endpoints require Modal and the Responses API, not chat completions
                                           Claude: --base-url URL [--endpoint-auth bearer|api-key] (default bearer)
                                           Claude gateways require Modal and support Claude models only; key from hidden input, stdin/file
                                           Endpoint prices: --price-model ID --price-input USD --price-output USD
                                           [--price-cached-input USD]; decimal USD per million tokens, omitted = unknown
                                           Replacing a connection replaces its prices too; re-enter all rates to keep them
  armada routine --help                    Create, inspect, enable, pause and manage scheduled template launches
  armada template --help                   Manage templates and launch published versions
  armada vars --help                       Readable variables: set/list/read; stdin/file writes only
  armada secrets --help                    Write-only application secrets: set/list/rotate/revoke/delete
                                           Personal or --org scope. JSON includes values only for vars read; no sandbox delivery.
  armada creds test <sandbox-id> --model <deployment-model> [--org <name>] [--json]
                                           One tiny billable check of the bound custom connection in a running Modal sandbox
  armada creds list [--org <name>]         Account IDs, names, defaults and safe metadata; never values
  armada creds rename <id> <name> [--org <name>]
  armada creds default|remove <id> [--org <name>]
  armada creds rm <kind> [--org <name>]
                                           kinds: claude-oauth, anthropic, openai, codex-auth, boat, modal, skillpack

Every command after 'armada login' acts as the user who signed in. snapshot commands take --org <name>
when you belong to more than one org.

Environment:
  ARMADA_TOKEN                             API token for non-interactive authentication. Takes precedence over
                                           a saved login session.
  ARMADA_URL                               Armada API server (default: https://armada.thevibecompany.co).
"
`;

exports[`armada api help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada api ROUTE [JSON|-]
POST to /api/v1/ROUTE using the selected Armada login and print the JSON response.
Pass the route relative to /api/v1/. With no body argument, send {}.
Pass - to read JSON from a pipe or redirected file. Use stdin for bodies that contain secrets.
Treat stdout as sensitive if the endpoint returns credentials.
Example: armada api org/list
"
`;

exports[`armada launch help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada launch <snapshot> --agent claude|codex|opencode ["prompt"] [options]
Fork a visible snapshot; a prompt starts the agent, otherwise wait for the first send.
Sign in with armada login or set ARMADA_TOKEN for scripts.
Find snapshots with armada snapshot list; select an organization with --org or armada org use.

Boat/Modal options:
  --provider boat|modal                  Select the snapshot provider.
  --model <id> --reasoning-effort <level> Model settings also apply to later turns.
  --credential-id UUID                   Model account; otherwise personal/org default.
  --repo <configured-dir|owner/repo>      Initial repository cwd, not confinement.
  --ttl <duration>                       Sandbox lifetime, for example 4h.
  --skill org/slug@version|slug@version   Repeat for multiple skills.
  --connections <id,...>                 Explicit MCP connections.
  --no-connections                       Opt out; omission uses server defaults.
  --keep-after-merge                     Disable automatic stopping after merged work.
  --armada-access                        Enable scoped Armada child access.
  --max-active-children <1..100>          Requires --armada-access.
  --org <org> --json                     Organization selector and machine output.

Docker form:
  armada launch [prompt] --provider docker --agent claude|codex|opencode [--org NAME|UUID]
    [--host UUID|NAME | --golden snapshot-UUID] [--credential-id UUID]
    [--repo owner/repo[@branch] ...] [--variable KEY:UUID:VERSION ...]
    [--secret KEY:UUID:VERSION ...] [--request-id UUID] [--ttl <duration>] [--json]
    [--model <id>] [--reasoning-effort <level>] [--credential-version <version>]
    [--skill org/slug@version|slug@version ...] [--connections <id,...> | --no-connections]
Uses the official Armada environment or an existing host-local snapshot.
A snapshot cannot be combined with --repo.
Boat supports Claude/Codex natively and OpenCode when the server runtime is installed.
OpenCode is also supported on Modal and Docker.
--golden is the existing Docker snapshot selector, not a new snapshot command.
Reuse a saved --request-id with identical input after an uncertain Docker outcome.

In an interactive terminal, bare armada launch opens the launcher.
Use explicit arguments in scripts; bare noninteractive launch is not supported.
--json emits the launch response on stdout without human launch advice.
After an uncertain launch, inspect armada list before retrying.
Example: armada launch starter --provider modal --agent codex "Inspect the repository" --json
Example: armada launch "Inspect the repository" --provider docker --host workstation --agent codex --org ORG_UUID
"
`;

exports[`armada list help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada list [--status <status>] [--agent <harness> | --harness <harness>]
  [--golden UUID] [--owner UUID|me] [--page <N>] [--limit <N>] [--org UUID] [--json] [--page-info]
List visible sandboxes after Armada login and server authorization.
Status: starting, ready, running, stopping, stopped, failed or deleted.
Deleted sandboxes are excluded by default; request --status deleted to include them.
--agent and --harness are aliases; use only one (claude, codex or opencode).
--golden filters by snapshot UUID. --owner accepts a user UUID or me.
Page is 1–1000000; limit is 1–200 (default page size 25). This is one page, not all results.
Human output is a table. --json returns {sandboxes:[...]}; --page-info returns full
page metadata and exact totals as JSON even without --json.
Use --org to override a saved selection. Reuse returned IDs with status/logs/send.
Example: armada list --owner me --page 1 --limit 25 --json
Example: armada list --org ORG_UUID --page 2 --page-info
"
`;

exports[`armada advice help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada advice <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
Read resource-sizing advice after Armada login and server authorization.
Collects one bounded sample and considers up to the last 10 comparable sessions.
Missing or partial evidence can produce no recommendation; do not treat unknowns as measured headroom.
This never resizes a running machine or changes defaults; recommendations concern future launches.
Human output summarizes supported advice or the no-recommendation reason.
Use --json to inspect the full evidence response. Use a sandbox ID from armada list.
Example: armada advice SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada rm help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada rm <sandbox-UUID> --yes [--org UUID] [--json]
Permanently delete a stopped sandbox after Armada login and server authorization.
Warning: deletion removes the disk AND recovery archives. --yes is required confirmation.
This is not stop. Inspect status and preserve needed work before deleting.
Use a sandbox UUID from armada list --json; names are not accepted.
Example: armada rm SANDBOX_UUID --yes --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada extend help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada extend <sandbox-UUID> --ttl <duration> [--org UUID] [--json]
Request a sandbox lifetime extension after Armada login and server authorization.
TTL is measured from now, not added to the old deadline, and must extend it.
Duration is a positive integer with optional s, m, h or d; a bare number is seconds.
Examples: 90, 90s, 30m, 4h, 2d. Server/provider state and lifetime limits still apply.
Use a sandbox UUID from armada list --json. Output is JSON even without --json.
Example: armada extend SANDBOX_UUID --ttl 30m --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada status help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada status <sandbox-id> [--live] [--org UUID] [--json]
Read sandbox status after Armada login and server authorization.
Default: recorded state, without a provider probe. --live reads provider/prompt details.
Use a sandbox ID from armada list --json. Output is JSON even without --json.
Recorded state is not proof of current provider readiness.
Example: armada status SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
Example: armada status SANDBOX_UUID --live --org ORG_UUID
"
`;

exports[`armada detail help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada detail <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
Read detailed sandbox information after Armada login and server authorization.
Use a sandbox ID from armada list --json. Output is JSON even without --json.
For recorded status without a provider probe, use armada status instead.
Example: armada detail SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada recovery help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada recovery <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
Read sandbox work-recovery information after Armada login and server authorization.
Output is JSON even without --json. This is not credential-account rebinding.
Use a sandbox ID from armada list --json.
Example: armada recovery SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada stop help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada stop <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
Stop the machine after Armada login and server authorization, not just the running turn.
Modal retains its disk only if checkpoint succeeds; fallback termination cannot resume.
Inspect the returned status/preservation note; do not assume disk retention.
After an uncertain outcome, inspect status/list before retrying. This is not permanent rm deletion.
Output is JSON even without --json.
Example: armada stop SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada interrupt help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada interrupt <sandbox-id> [--org UUID] [--json]
Interrupt the sandbox's running turn after Armada login and authorization.
The sandbox stays up for the next send. This does not stop or delete the machine.
Availability depends on the provider/harness; the server can refuse unsupported interruption.
Output is JSON even without --json.
Example: armada interrupt SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada wait help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada wait <sandbox-id> [--timeout <seconds>] [--org UUID] [--json]
Wait for sandbox activity through the server after Armada login and authorization.
Timeout is an integer from 0 to 600 seconds (default 60); zero is accepted.
Use a sandbox ID from armada list --json. Output is JSON even without --json.
Inspect the returned state; a bounded wait is not proof that your task is complete.
Example: armada wait SANDBOX_UUID --timeout 60 --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada report help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada report <sandbox-id> ready|blocked --prompt-id <id> [--org UUID] [--json]
  ready: [--pr-number <N>] [--pr-url <URL>] [--head-sha <SHA>]
  blocked: --reason <text>
Report an explicit outcome for the latest finished turn after Armada login.
Only the sandbox owner or current organization admin may report.
Call after wait; use the latest finished prompt ID, not an arbitrary earlier turn.
A new send clears the outcome. Message text alone is not a reported outcome.
Blocked requires a nonempty reason (maximum 1000 characters).
Ready accepts optional PR metadata; the report itself does not verify CI or merge a PR.
Output is JSON even without --json.
Example: armada report SANDBOX_UUID blocked --prompt-id PROMPT_ID --reason "Need owner input"
Example: armada report SANDBOX_UUID ready --prompt-id PROMPT_ID --pr-number 123
"
`;

exports[`armada send help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada send <sandbox-id> <text> [--model <id>] [--reasoning-effort <level>]
  [--request-id UUID] [--org UUID] [--json]
Send a new instruction after Armada login and server authorization.
Use steer to redirect a running turn. Retained stopped sandboxes can wake on send.
Native model/effort overrides apply to this turn only; runtime model/effort remain launch-pinned.
For uncertain runtime delivery, reuse the request ID with identical input.
Native providers do not deduplicate by request ID; inspect state before retrying.
Output is JSON; human mode also reports acceptance progress on stderr.
Put options before -- when the literal text contains option-like words.
Example: armada send SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID -- "Review the changes"
"
`;

exports[`armada steer help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada steer <sandbox-id> <text> [--org UUID] [--json]
Redirect a currently running turn after Armada login and server authorization.
Refused when no turn is running; use send for a new turn.
Delivery may join the turn natively or interrupt/restart the same conversation;
inspect the returned delivery field. Output is JSON even without --json.
Put options before -- to pass literal option-like text.
Example: armada steer SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID -- "Focus on the failing test"
"
`;

exports[`armada exec help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada exec <sandbox-id> <command> [--cwd <path>] [--timeout <seconds>] [--org UUID] [--json]
Run a shell command after Armada login and server authorization; not an agent prompt.
Default cwd is /home/user. Timeout is integer seconds 1–600 (default 120).
Output is JSON with command exitCode, stdout/stderr and timeout/truncation information.
Inspect exitCode and timedOut; a successful CLI request is not proof the shell command succeeded.
Put options before -- so command flags are passed literally, not parsed as Armada options.
Example: armada exec SANDBOX_UUID --org ORG_UUID -- "git status --short"
"
`;

exports[`armada logs help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Read agent events from a sandbox.

Usage:
  armada logs <sandbox-id> [--since <cursor>] [--all] [--follow] [--org <org>] [--json]
  armada logs <sandbox-id> --tail [n] [--follow] [--org <org>] [--json]

Options:
  --since <cursor>  Resume after an opaque cursor returned by a JSON page.
  --tail [n]        Last n events (1–1000; default 100). You cannot combine
                    --tail with --all or --since. With --follow, tail is read once.
  --all             Drain all currently available pages, without polling delays.
  --follow          Drain pages, then poll while active until settled.
                    Ctrl-C stops only this reader, not the agent.
  --org <org>       Override the organization selected by armada org use.
  --json            Stream page objects: events, cursor, hasMore, settled.
                    Multiple pages are not one JSON array. Errors use stderr.

Without --all, --follow or --tail, read one bounded page.
Use armada list --json to find a visible sandbox ID.

Examples:
  armada logs SANDBOX_ID --tail 20
  armada logs SANDBOX_ID --since CURSOR --follow
  armada logs SANDBOX_ID --all --json | jq -c '.events[]'

For other commands and authentication settings: armada --help.
"
`;

exports[`armada routine help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada routine <command> [arguments]
Manage scheduled template launches that run as the routine owner.

  create  Create a paused routine from file or inline configuration.
  list    List visible IDs, names, status, versions and next occurrences.
  show    Inspect configuration, prompt and current version.
  update  Replace configuration from file, or change only the inline schedule.
  enable  Schedule the next occurrence, not an immediate manual launch.
  pause   Stop new admissions; already admitted runs may finish.
  delete  Mark deleted; does not stop or delete running sandboxes.
  runs    Read the latest 100 decisions and original-prompt outcomes.

Actions require Armada login or ARMADA_TOKEN. Select an organization with --org
NAME|UUID or armada org use. Organization-scoped writes require administrator permission.
Use IDs from routine list when exact names collide; keep IDs for history after deletion.
Updates and state changes require --expected-version from routine show/list;
conflicts are not retried automatically. Prompts are readable configuration, not secrets.
Schedules use five-field cron and IANA time zones, with runs at least one hour apart.
Launch success is not task success; unknown is not success. Inspect routine runs.
Inspect a command's options with armada routine <command> --help.
Example: armada routine list --org ORG_UUID --json
Example: armada routine runs ROUTINE_UUID --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada template help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada template <command> [arguments]
Manage reusable launch definitions and their immutable template versions.

  create   Create a template from a JSON definition file or definition flags.
  list     List visible IDs, names, scopes, status and current versions.
  get      Inspect metadata or a selected version's definition with --json.
  publish  Publish a complete new version, guarded by --expected-revision.
  delete   Mark a template deleted, guarded by --expected-revision.
  launch   Launch a template version using a saved --request-key for safe retries.

Actions require Armada login or ARMADA_TOKEN. Select an organization with --org
NAME|UUID or armada org use. Templates may be personal or organization-scoped;
organization-scoped writes require administrator permission.
Use IDs from template list when exact names collide across scopes.
Definitions store variable/secret references; do not put raw secret values in them.
References resolve into frozen manifests at launch, not publication.
After an unknown launch outcome, keep the same request key and body; do not start
a new attempt. See template launch --help for resolved-ID recovery.
Inspect a command's full options with armada template <command> --help.
Example: armada template list --org ORG_UUID --json
Example: armada template get TEMPLATE_UUID --revision 1 --org ORG_UUID --json
"
`;

exports[`armada snapshot help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada snapshot <command> [arguments]
Manage provider-scoped snapshots. Use a leaf command with --help or -h for syntax.
Boat/Modal:
  create       Build a snapshot from repositories/config.
  update       Append repositories or explicitly replace the complete config.
  build-find   Find an active build by name.
  build-status Inspect a job or recover admission using its saved key.
  build-cancel Cancel your queued, never-started build.
  capabilities Read server-authoritative capacity.
  size-policy  Inspect or replace future-launch size defaults.
  remove       Remove from launch selection, retaining children/provider artifacts.
  visibility   Change visibility using the snapshot record ID.
  retirement dry-run|reconcile  Review history and reconcile reviewed receipts.
Docker:
  create       Build a host-local snapshot (--provider docker, host/org required).
  get          Read saved build state.
  rebuild      Build a new snapshot on the original host, keeping the old one.
Shared:
  list         List visible snapshots; Docker requires an organization UUID.
Operations require Armada login and server authorization; help does not.
The legacy golden spelling is accepted; guidance uses snapshot.
Example: armada snapshot list --provider modal --json
Example: armada snapshot create --help
"
`;

exports[`armada snapshot create help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada snapshot create <name> (--repo <url>[@branch] ... | --config <file>)
  [--init <command> ...] [--setup <local-file>] [--provider boat|modal]
  [--private] [--org <org>] [--idempotency-key <key>] [--timeout <seconds>] [--json]
Docker: armada snapshot create <name> --provider docker --host UUID|NAME --org UUID
  (--config <file> | --repo <url>[@branch] ... [--init <command> ...])
  [--private] [--request-id UUID] [--json]
--config cannot be combined with --repo or --init; include init commands in the JSON file.
Docker builds a host-local snapshot; --setup and durable-job flags above are Boat/Modal only.
Boat/Modal --timeout defaults to 60 seconds (0 returns after admission, maximum 600).
Save --idempotency-key to recover uncertain admission with snapshot build-status.
Requires Armada login, a connected provider and GitHub repository authorization.
Builds, scans and snapshots on the server; do not include secrets in setup.
Example: armada snapshot create starter --provider modal --repo https://github.com/octocat/Hello-World --org my-team
"
`;

exports[`armada snapshot retirement reconcile help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada snapshot retirement reconcile <snapshot-UUID> [--file <path>] [--limit <1-50>] [--org UUID] [--json]
Reconcile reviewed history and enqueue eligible retirement work after Armada login and authorization.
Supply reviewed dry-run JSON via stdin or --file; do not invent receipts.
The JSON must contain 1–50 valid receipts in results. Limit defaults to 20.
Omit --file for stdin; --file - is not the stdin spelling for this command.
Server rechecks authority, history and references; eligible work is queued, not proof of deletion.
Output is always JSON. Use the snapshot record UUID, not a provider artifact ID.
Example: armada snapshot retirement reconcile SNAPSHOT_UUID --org ORG_UUID --file reviewed.json
"
`;

exports[`armada golden retirement dry-run help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada snapshot retirement dry-run <snapshot-UUID> [--limit <1-50>] [--org UUID] [--json]
Review bounded historical snapshot retirement candidates after Armada login.
Requires server owner/admin authorization. Limit defaults to 20; output is always JSON.
Use the snapshot record UUID, not a sandbox ID or provider artifact ID.
Review the results and receipts before using retirement reconcile. No provider mutation in this review.
Example: armada snapshot retirement dry-run SNAPSHOT_UUID --org ORG_UUID --limit 20
"
`;

exports[`armada creds help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada creds <command> [arguments]
Manage encrypted model/provider credentials, not application variables/secrets.
Use a leaf command with --help or -h for its flags and secure input instructions.
Native subscription accounts:
  login codex  Authorize native Codex in your browser; keep the terminal open.
  import       Import an existing Claude token or Codex auth JSON securely.
API connections:
  presets      List built-in provider choices without login.
  connect      Store a named API connection using a preset.
  set          Store a supported credential kind or custom endpoint.
  test         Test a sandbox's bound model connection; may incur a provider charge.
Account metadata:
  list         List metadata/IDs, never credential values.
  rename       Rename by account ID.
  default      Select a default account by ID.
  remove       Remove an account by ID.
  rm           Legacy kind-scoped removal; not an alias for remove <id>.
Native subscriptions require eligible subscription access; API keys use provider billing.
Read secret inputs from stdin/hidden input or private files, never command arguments.
For application environment values use vars or secrets instead. Server authorization still applies.
Example: armada creds presets --json
Example: armada creds import --help
"
`;

exports[`armada creds import claude help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada creds import claude (--name <name> | --id <id> --expected-version <version>) [--file <path>] [--org <name>]
Import an existing native credential after Armada login; no native login is started.
Claude: run claude setup-token locally. Codex: supply native auth JSON.
Read the credential from stdin/hidden input or a private --file, never command arguments.
Use --name to create; replacement requires --id and a positive integer --expected-version.
Omit --org for personal scope; pass it for organization scope.
Credentials are stored encrypted; allowance information may be unavailable.
Example: armada creds import claude --name personal --file /private/native-credential
"
`;

exports[`armada creds connect help preserves its output 1`] = `
"Usage: armada creds connect <preset> --name <name> [--file <path>] [--org <name>] [--json]
  [--models <id,...>] [--base-url <HTTPS-url>] [--api-version <version>]
  [--max-agents <positive-integer> | --unlimited]
Store a named connection after Armada login. Choose a preset with armada creds presets --json.
Supply only the API key through stdin/hidden input or a private --file, never command arguments or JSON.
Presets with harness models reject --models, --base-url and --api-version.
Chosen-model presets require --models and, unless fixed by the preset, --base-url.
Fixed-address presets reject --base-url; --api-version is only accepted by supporting presets.
Official Azure addresses are tested with a tiny billable request per deployment before saving;
a failed check saves nothing. Omit --org for personal scope.
Morph defaults to 2 simultaneous agents; other accounts default to unlimited.
Use --max-agents N or --unlimited on connect, import, login and set.
Change an existing cap with armada creds limits ACCOUNT_UUID --max-agents 2 or --unlimited.
Morph: armada creds connect morph --name shared --file /private/morph-key
Use Claude Code, Codex or OpenCode on Modal, Docker or runtime-backed Boat.
At launch choose --credential-id ACCOUNT_UUID and a Morph plan model with --model.
Example: armada creds connect openai --name personal --file /private/api-key
"
`;
